cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "feasible-rerank: fair, energy-aware recommendation list selection via QUBO"
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Huzaifa
    family-names: Abdul Rehman
repository-code: "https://github.com/HuzaifaAbdulRehman/feasible-rerank"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - recommender-systems
  - quantum-annealing
  - QUBO
  - fairness
  - green-computing
abstract: >-
  Selects a k-item recommendation list by minimising a QUBO over relevance,
  intra-list diversity and group exposure fairness, with exact-k enforced as a
  penalty term, and reports the energy cost of doing so.

  Six findings are documented. First, the textbook recipe -- penalty-encoded
  cardinality handed to a single-flip simulated annealer -- fails silently at
  realistic candidate-set sizes: two feasible k-sets are never adjacent under
  single-spin-flip moves, so the sampler returns lists that are always the right
  length and close to arbitrary among lists of that length, losing to a greedy MMR
  baseline on its own objective. Tabu search and a constraint-preserving swap
  annealer both recover roughly a factor of two.

  Second, the failure is a property of the encoding rather than of thermal search: a
  continuous-dynamics solver (Simulated Bifurcation) fails on the same problem by a
  different mechanism, and no amount of additional compute rescues either.

  Third, the choice of the diversity and fairness weights decides the QUBO-versus-
  classical comparison by a larger margin than the methods differ from one another; a
  comparison run with the fairness term disabled reverses the conclusion.

  Fourth, and correcting an earlier version of this work: the formulation's advantage is
  not feasibility. A textbook largest-remainder apportionment baseline attains the same
  exposure-fairness budget on all eight benchmarks, deterministically and at a small
  fraction of the compute (no multiplier is quoted: the timing columns were recorded on a
  contended machine and await a clean sequential measurement). The earlier claim that no classical reranker could meet the constraint
  measured a missing remainder rule in one quota heuristic rather than any property of
  classical reranking, and was refuted by a fifteen-line baseline.

  Fifth, what survives is smaller and about accuracy. At that shared tightest budget the
  QUBO returns a more accurate list than the apportionment baseline on eight of eight
  benchmarks -- mean +0.0086 NDCG, sign test and Wilcoxon both p = 0.008 -- with the
  largest margin where the similarity matrix is densest. The mechanism is
  non-separability: apportionment fills each group greedily, which is optimal for a
  separable objective and not for one carrying a pairwise diversity term, and the QUBO's
  margin on the objective grows with that term's weight.

  Sixth, the naive quota heuristic collapses entirely where exposure targets are
  proportional rather than equal, meeting no budget below 1.00 on real product
  categories, while both the apportionment baseline and the QUBO reach 0.20. A matched
  ablation changing only the target vector restores it, isolating round-robin integer
  quotas as the cause.
